{
  "rules": [
    "Small positive bias at short horizons (≈days 1–10): model is typically ~0.1–0.6 EUR too low during rising phases; apply a mild upward offset when the last 5–10 days trend is positive.",
    "Persistent overprediction at mid horizons (≈days 12–25): model tends to sit ~0.5–2.0 EUR above realized prices during the subsequent pullback/mean-reversion; damp forecasts toward the recent 10–20d mean when momentum weakens.",
    "Turning-point lag: after local peaks, the model declines too slowly (keeps prices elevated), and after local troughs it rebounds too slowly; increase mean-reversion strength when recent volatility is high.",
    "Spike handling: following sharp upside moves, the model often underestimates the immediate continuation (next ~1 week) but then overshoots by projecting the elevated level too far out; use a two-stage adjustment—boost days 1–7 slightly, but cap/dampen days 10–25.",
    "Late-horizon underestimation in renewed rallies (≈days 26–30): when the market re-accelerates upward, the model can be several EUR too low; add a trend-following uplift to the tail when the last ~10 days slope is strongly positive."
  ]
}
